  "response": "After its publication, the name polywater spread rapidly through both the scientific literature and the popular press. The liquid existed only in quantities of micrograms, far too little for conventional bulk analysis, so every reported property had to be inferred from instruments working near the limits of their sensitivity. That report confirms the second of these readings. Whereas Fedyakin had worked in Leningrad, the research gained its international standing in Moscow. The polywater samples contained silica dissolved from the walls of the capillaries, or, as Rousseau's sweat spectrum indicates, they contained organic contaminants such as lactate from human sweat. Whether polywater was a new substance or a contaminated sample was decided by the convergence of three lines of inquiry: spectroscopy, chemical analysis of the samples, and the final judgement of the researchers who had championed the substance.\n\nThe substance was also reported to be denser and more viscous than ordinary water, with a markedly reduced vapor pressure. Once the leading proponents themselves attributed the anomalous behaviour to impurities, the polymer model no longer had any defenders, and Rousseau's spectrum could be accepted for what it showed. Fedyakin conducted his early anomalous water experiments in Leningrad. On this point the record allows only two readings, which cannot both be true, and one of which must hold: either Deryagin publicly retracted his claim that anomalous water was a new form of water, or he never retracted it at all. Lippincott was a chemist at Harvard University. The episode began in the Soviet Union, where Nikolai Fedyakin first reported anomalous water condensed in narrow glass capillaries in 1962.\n\nAlthough Lippincott's group had proposed that polymeric structure, Denis Rousseau obtained an infrared spectrum resembling polywater from his own sweat, which he collected after a game of handball. The properties that Deryagin's laboratory described seemed to require a new structural explanation. Taken together, the evidence from all three lines of inquiry indicated contamination, and polywater came to be understood as ordinary water containing dissolved impurities rather than as a new form of water. The anomalous water was produced by condensing water vapor in fine quartz or glass capillaries. Whereas the silica was a feature of the samples themselves, the remaining question concerned Deryagin's own position. The difficulty was one of scale.\n\nThat Science paper resulted from his group's proposal that anomalous water was a polymer of water molecules, a network bound together by unusually strong hydrogen bonds. The decisive document is the report in which Deryagin and Churaev concluded, in Nature in 1973, that the anomalous properties were caused by impurities. Anomalous water froze at a lower temperature than ordinary water; in other words, ordinary water froze at a higher temperature than anomalous water. Some time after that 1962 report, Boris Deryagin of the Institute of Physical Chemistry in Moscow took up and publicized the anomalous water research, giving it the authority of one of the most respected surface chemists of his generation. Microprobe and mass-spectrometric studies supported this picture: they detected sodium, silicon, and organic residues in concentrations far exceeding anything that pure water could contain.\n\nUnder that constraint, a small amount of an unnoticed foreign substance could dominate the behaviour of the sample, and this possibility shaped the entire controversy. It also removes the earlier tension between the polymer proposal and the sweat spectrum. Laboratories in the West soon took up the problem, and Ellis Lippincott and colleagues published infrared spectra of anomalous water in Science in 1969. The procedure appeared clean, because the source water had been distilled and the capillaries were freshly drawn. Direct chemical analysis of the samples led to the same conclusion, because the anomalous material was contaminated from at least one of two sources, perhaps both. Spectroscopy, however, could also be used to test the polymer model.",
